## useContext

## Setup

```tsx
// 1. Create context with a default value
const ThemeContext = createContext<'light' | 'dark'>('light');

// 2. Provide it high in the tree
function App() {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState<'light' | 'dark'>('light');
  return (
    <ThemeContext.Provider value={theme}>
      <Layout />
    </ThemeContext.Provider>
  );
}

// 3. Consume anywhere below
function Button() {
  const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);
  return <button className={`btn btn--${theme}`}>Click</button>;
}
```

## vs Context.Consumer

```tsx
// Old: Consumer wrapper (verbose)
<ThemeContext.Consumer>
  {theme => <button className={theme}>Click</button>}
</ThemeContext.Consumer>

// Modern: useContext (clean)
const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);
```

## Good Use Cases

- Auth user / session
- Theme (light/dark)
- Locale / i18n
- Feature flags
- Toast / notification system

## When NOT to Use Context

Context triggers a re-render in **every consumer** when the value changes — even if the consumer only reads one field of a large object. For high-frequency updates (mouse position, form input), prefer Zustand, Jotai, or component-local state. Split contexts by update frequency to limit blast radius.

## useState vs useReducer

## useState — Simple Cases

```tsx
const [open, setOpen]       = useState(false);
const [query, setQuery]     = useState('');
const [count, setCount]     = useState(0);
```

## useReducer — Complex Cases

```tsx
type State = { loading: boolean; data: User[] | null; error: string | null; };
type Action =
  | { type: 'FETCH_START' }
  | { type: 'FETCH_SUCCESS'; payload: User[] }
  | { type: 'FETCH_ERROR'; payload: string };

function reducer(state: State, action: Action): State {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'FETCH_START':   return { ...state, loading: true, error: null };
    case 'FETCH_SUCCESS': return { loading: false, data: action.payload, error: null };
    case 'FETCH_ERROR':   return { loading: false, data: null, error: action.payload };
  }
}

const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, { loading: false, data: null, error: null });

// Usage
dispatch({ type: 'FETCH_START' });
```

## Decision Guide

| Scenario | Pick |
|---|---|
| 1–2 independent booleans/strings | `useState` |
| Object with fields that change together | `useReducer` |
| Next state depends on previous | `useReducer` (functional update also works) |
| 3+ related event types | `useReducer` |
| Need testable state logic | `useReducer` — pure function, no component needed |

## Testing a Reducer

```ts
it('handles FETCH_SUCCESS', () => {
  const next = reducer({ loading: true, data: null, error: null }, {
    type: 'FETCH_SUCCESS', payload: [mockUser]
  });
  expect(next.loading).toBe(false);
  expect(next.data).toHaveLength(1);
});
```

## useRef Use Cases

## 1. DOM Access

```tsx
const inputRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);

useEffect(() => { inputRef.current?.focus(); }, []);

return <input ref={inputRef} />;
```

## 2. Persist Values Without Re-render

Unlike state, mutating `ref.current` does not cause a re-render:

```tsx
// Store previous value
function usePrevious<T>(value: T) {
  const ref = useRef<T | undefined>(undefined);
  useEffect(() => { ref.current = value; }); // update after render
  return ref.current;
}

// Store timer ID
const timerRef = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setInterval> | null>(null);
useEffect(() => {
  timerRef.current = setInterval(tick, 1000);
  return () => { if (timerRef.current) clearInterval(timerRef.current); };
}, []);
```

## 3. Abort Controller for Cancellable Fetches

```tsx
const abortRef = useRef<AbortController | null>(null);

useEffect(() => {
  abortRef.current?.abort(); // cancel previous
  abortRef.current = new AbortController();

  fetch('/api/data', { signal: abortRef.current.signal })
    .then(r => r.json())
    .then(setData)
    .catch(err => { if (err.name !== 'AbortError') setError(err); });

  return () => abortRef.current?.abort();
}, [query]);
```

## 4. Instance Variables (Class Equivalent)

In class components, `this.x` held mutable instance data. `useRef` is the functional equivalent — persists for the component's lifetime, mutable, no re-render on change.

## useState vs useRef

## Core Difference

| | `useState` | `useRef` |
|---|---|---|
| Triggers re-render? | Yes | No |
| Value survives re-render? | Yes | Yes |
| Mutable? | Via setter only | Direct mutation (`ref.current = x`) |
| Visible to JSX? | Yes | Only if you read `.current` |

## Example: Counter

```tsx
// useState — UI reflects value
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
// <p>{count}</p> updates on screen

// useRef — value tracked internally, UI never updates
const renderCount = useRef(0);
useEffect(() => { renderCount.current += 1; }); // silent mutation
```

## Stale Closure with useState

```tsx
const [query, setQuery] = useState('');

useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => {
    console.log(query); // stale — captures initial value
  }, 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []); // missing dep

// Fix: use ref to always read latest
const queryRef = useRef(query);
useEffect(() => { queryRef.current = query; }, [query]);

useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => {
    console.log(queryRef.current); // always fresh
  }, 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);
```

## Rule of Thumb

> If the UI must show the value → `useState`. If it's internal bookkeeping → `useRef`.

## useMemo

## Syntax

```tsx
const memoizedValue = useMemo(() => computeExpensiveValue(a, b), [a, b]);
```

The factory function only re-runs when `a` or `b` changes.

## When to Use

### 1. Expensive Computation

```tsx
const filteredList = useMemo(
  () => largeList.filter(item => item.name.includes(query)).sort(compareFn),
  [largeList, query]
);
```

### 2. Stable Reference for Memoized Children

```tsx
// Without useMemo: new array reference every render → MyList always re-renders
// With useMemo: same reference if deps unchanged → React.memo bails out
const items = useMemo(() => data.map(transformItem), [data]);

return <MyList items={items} />; // MyList = React.memo(MyList)
```

### 3. Stable Dependency for Another Hook

```tsx
const options = useMemo(() => ({ method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(payload) }), [payload]);

useEffect(() => {
  fetch('/api', options);
}, [options]); // won't re-run unless payload changes
```

## When NOT to Use

- Cheap calculations — `useMemo` itself has overhead (closure, dependency comparison).
- As a default — don't wrap everything. Profile first.
- Instead of fixing a structural problem — if a component always re-renders unnecessarily, fix the cause.

## useMemo vs useCallback

`useMemo` memoizes a **value**. `useCallback` memoizes a **function**. `useCallback(fn, deps)` is equivalent to `useMemo(() => fn, deps)`.

## useCallback Use Cases

## Syntax

```tsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
  doSomething(id);
}, [id]);
```

## 1. Memoized Child Components

```tsx
const Row = React.memo(({ onDelete }: { onDelete: (id: string) => void }) => (
  <button onClick={() => onDelete(rowId)}>Delete</button>
));

function List({ items }: { items: Item[] }) {
  // Without useCallback: new function ref every render → Row always re-renders
  const handleDelete = useCallback((id: string) => {
    setItems(prev => prev.filter(i => i.id !== id));
  }, []); // stable — no deps that change

  return items.map(item => <Row key={item.id} onDelete={handleDelete} />);
}
```

## 2. Stable useEffect Dependency

```tsx
const fetchData = useCallback(async () => {
  const data = await api.get(url);
  setData(data);
}, [url]);

useEffect(() => {
  fetchData();
}, [fetchData]); // re-runs only when url changes
```

## 3. Native Event Listeners

```tsx
const handleResize = useCallback(() => {
  setWidth(window.innerWidth);
}, []);

useEffect(() => {
  window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);
  return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', handleResize);
}, [handleResize]); // stable ref — listener added/removed once
```

## When NOT to Use

- Inline handlers that don't flow to memoized children.
- When the child doesn't use `React.memo` — memoizing the callback achieves nothing.
- Prematurely — profile before optimising.

## React.memo vs useMemo

## React.memo — Memoize a Component

```tsx
// Skips re-render if props are shallowly equal
const Avatar = React.memo(({ user }: { user: User }) => (
  <img src={user.avatar} alt={user.name} />
));

// Custom comparison
const Avatar = React.memo(
  ({ user }) => <img src={user.avatar} />,
  (prev, next) => prev.user.id === next.user.id // only re-render if id changes
);
```

## useMemo — Memoize a Value

```tsx
function ProductList({ products, category }: Props) {
  // Only recomputes when products or category changes
  const filtered = useMemo(
    () => products.filter(p => p.category === category),
    [products, category]
  );

  return filtered.map(p => <ProductCard key={p.id} product={p} />);
}
```

## How They Work Together

```tsx
const ExpensiveChild = React.memo(({ items }: { items: Item[] }) => (
  <ul>{items.map(i => <li key={i.id}>{i.name}</li>)}</ul>
));

function Parent({ rawData, filter }: Props) {
  // useMemo: stable array reference
  const items = useMemo(() => rawData.filter(i => i.type === filter), [rawData, filter]);

  // React.memo: child bails out if items reference is stable
  return <ExpensiveChild items={items} />;
}
```

## Summary

| | What it memoizes | Where |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | Component render output | Wrap the component definition |
| `useMemo` | Computed value | Inside a component |

## Hooks vs HOCs vs Render Props

## The Problem They All Solve

Reusing stateful logic across components — e.g., window size, data fetching, auth state.

## HOC Approach (verbose, tree pollution)

```tsx
function withUser<P>(Component: React.ComponentType<P & { user: User }>) {
  return function WithUser(props: P) {
    const user = useUserFromSomewhere();
    return <Component {...props} user={user} />;
  };
}
const PageWithUser = withUser(Page);
// DevTools shows: WithUser > Page — hard to debug
```

## Render Props (callback hell)

```tsx
<UserProvider render={(user) => <Page user={user} />} />
// Nested render props create "callback hell" in JSX
```

## Custom Hook (clean)

```tsx
function useUser() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState<User | null>(null);
  useEffect(() => { fetchCurrentUser().then(setUser); }, []);
  return user;
}

function Page() {
  const user = useUser(); // no wrapper, no extra component
  return <h1>Hello, {user?.name}</h1>;
}
```

## When HOCs Still Make Sense

- **Error boundaries** — still require class components; HOC wrapping is idiomatic.
- **Library interop** — some libraries (react-redux's `connect`, older Formik) expose HOC APIs.
- **Code requiring class lifecycle** — rare in modern React.

## Rule of Thumb

Default to a custom hook. Reach for a HOC only when the consuming component needs no API changes and you're wrapping for cross-cutting concerns.

## What Hooks Don't Cover

## 1. Error Boundaries

`componentDidCatch` and `getDerivedStateFromError` have no hook equivalent — you must use a class component:

```tsx
class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component<
  { fallback: React.ReactNode; children: React.ReactNode },
  { hasError: boolean }
> {
  state = { hasError: false };

  static getDerivedStateFromError() {
    return { hasError: true };
  }

  componentDidCatch(error: Error, info: React.ErrorInfo) {
    logError(error, info);
  }

  render() {
    return this.state.hasError ? this.props.fallback : this.props.children;
  }
}
```

Libraries like `react-error-boundary` wrap this into a convenient component so you never have to write it yourself.

## 2. getSnapshotBeforeUpdate

Captures a value (e.g., scroll position) from the DOM before React applies updates. No hook runs at the exact same moment. `useLayoutEffect` is the closest alternative but runs after the DOM update.

## Hook Coverage Matrix

| Class Feature | Hook Equivalent |
|---|---|
| `this.state` / `setState` | `useState` / `useReducer` |
| `componentDidMount` | `useEffect(() => {}, [])` |
| `componentDidUpdate` | `useEffect(() => {}, [deps])` |
| `componentWillUnmount` | `useEffect` cleanup return |
| `shouldComponentUpdate` | `React.memo` |
| `getDerivedStateFromProps` | Derive in render body |
| `getSnapshotBeforeUpdate` | Partial via `useLayoutEffect` |
| `componentDidCatch` | **No equivalent — class required** |
